"""Curated Photoshop 27.11 Windows learning content. Single editorial source of truth."""
STAGES = [
('Orientation & image fundamentals','See files, pixels, resolution and the interface before editing.'),
('Documents, layers & geometry','Build safely editable files and navigate space.'),
('Selections, masks & isolation','Control precisely where changes appear.'),
('Nondestructive editing','Preserve source pixels and remain revision-ready.'),
('Retouching & restoration','Remove distractions and repair images without damaging texture.'),
('Painting & brushes','Understand strokes, color, brush behavior and fills.'),
('Typography, shapes & paths','Build readable layouts and precise vector-edged components.'),
('Photography & color','Make principled tonal, color, RAW and output decisions.'),
('Advanced compositing','Integrate disparate source elements believably.'),
('Professional production','Deliver many assets reliably with automation and collaboration.'),
('Performance & troubleshooting','Diagnose failures, bottlenecks and virtualized setups.'),
('Mastery & capstones','Apply the entire skill system with evidence-based review.')]
LESSONS=[]
def L(stage, title, concept, location, steps, expected, why, practice, proof, mistake, tip, question, answers, correct=0, note='', use='', prerequisite=None):
    id=f's{stage:02d}l{1+sum(x["stage"]==stage for x in LESSONS):02d}'
    prev=LESSONS[-1]['id'] if LESSONS else None
    LESSONS.append(dict(id=id,stage=stage,title=title,objective='Explain and independently execute '+title.lower()+'.',concept=concept,location=location,
       steps=[x.strip() for x in steps.split('~')],expected=expected,why=why,practice=practice,proof=proof,mistake=mistake,tip=tip,
       question=question,answers=answers.split('~'),correct=correct,explanation=note or why,use=use or 'Use this whenever a document must remain clear, reproducible, and easy to revise.',prerequisites=prerequisite if prerequisite is not None else ([prev] if prev else [])))

# Stage 1
L(1,'What Photoshop edits: pixels versus vectors', 'A raster image is a grid of colored samples. Pixel dimensions limit recoverable detail; vector paths represent mathematically defined contours, although Photoshop eventually rasterizes the display/export. A photo can contain editable vector text and shapes in the same PSD.','File > New; Window > Layers', 'Create a 600 × 400 px, RGB Color, 8-bit, white document.~Zoom to 400% and observe the square edges of a painted pixel.~Select Rectangle Tool in Shape mode, draw a rectangle, then zoom in.~Inspect the new shape layer and compare its editable edges with raster painting.', 'A shape layer remains a separate editable object; painted pixels show discrete squares at high zoom.', 'Raster images sample color at finite coordinates; vectors describe geometry independently of that grid.', 'Create one raster mark and one Shape layer, rename both and compare them at 800% zoom.', 'Explain which artwork tolerates repeated scaling and why.', 'Do not confuse zooming the view with increasing real resolution.', 'Use Shape mode rather than Pixels mode when you want editable geometry.', 'Which is resolution-independent before export?', 'A vector path~A flattened JPEG~A raster brushstroke',0)
L(1,'Pixels, dimensions, PPI and print size','Pixel width × pixel height determines image samples; PPI is metadata about their physical placement, not a magic detail generator. For example 3000 pixels at 300 PPI spans 10 inches at native size. Resampling changes the pixel count.','Image > Image Size','Open or create a 1200 × 800 px document.~Choose Image > Image Size and disable Resample.~Change Resolution from 72 to 300 Pixels/Inch; observe pixel dimensions.~Enable Resample, change width to 600 pixels, and compare the dimensions again.','With Resample off, only print dimensions change; with it on, the number of pixels changes.','Resampling estimates samples, whereas PPI without resampling only changes the conversion between pixels and physical units.','Find the print width of a 2400 px image at 300 PPI without a calculator dialog.','Correct answer is 8 inches; keep 2400 original pixels.', 'Blindly typing 300 PPI does not improve detail in a small JPEG.', 'Keep Resample off while testing print-size relationships.', 'What does changing PPI with Resample disabled alter?','Physical print size only~Pixel width~Number of layers',0)
L(1,'Create, open, save and close safely','A layered PSD or large PSB is a working master; JPEG is a usually flattened, lossy delivery copy; PNG can retain alpha but is not a layered PSD replacement. Work on copies and save an editable source.','File > New; File > Open; File > Save As; File > Save a Copy','Create a 1000 × 800 px RGB document, then paint a mark on a new layer.~Choose File > Save As and save a local PSD named 01-master.psd.~Reopen it and verify the Layers panel contains the painted layer.~Use File > Save a Copy to create a JPEG or PNG delivery file; reopen separately.','The PSD retains distinct layers; an exported flattened file is for distribution.','Working and delivery files have different jobs; reversible structure belongs in the master.','Make a folder named project and save master.psd plus a delivery.png.','Reopen master.psd and demonstrate that the mark can be independently hidden.','Do not overwrite the only layered master with a flattened format.', 'Use revision suffixes or versioned folders for critical deliverables.', 'What should be kept for revising layers later?','PSD master~JPEG only~Screenshot',0)
L(1,'Interface, panels, workspaces and preferences','The Toolbar chooses an operation; the Options bar changes its parameters; panels expose the document structure and adjustment controls. A workspace stores panel layout, not image pixels. Shortcuts may be intercepted by text fields.','Window > Workspace; Window > Layers; Window > Properties; Edit > Preferences > General','Open Window > Layers and Window > Properties.~Drag a panel tab to rearrange the workspace; restore it using Window > Workspace > Reset Essentials (when Essentials is active).~Select Edit > Preferences > General and inspect settings without changing unknown options.~Click inside and outside a numeric field; test a tool key only when focus is off the field.','Panels can be repositioned without changing the image; keyboard focus affects commands.','UI layout is separate from document content, so workspace changes are safe to experiment with.','Configure a workspace with Layers, Properties and History visible; restore Essentials.','Locate both panels and show the default layout again.','If a shortcut types a letter into a field, click the canvas or press Esc to exit the field.', 'Save a custom workspace only after its layout is useful.', 'Which choice restores a workspace layout?','Window > Workspace > Reset Essentials~Image > Mode~File > Export',0)
L(1,'Zoom, pan, rulers and guides','View zoom changes screen magnification, not pixel content. Rulers, guides and the Info panel help measure and align without altering artwork. A hand-pan does not move document layers.','View > Rulers; View > New Guide; Window > Info; Zoom Tool; Hand Tool','Open a document and press Ctrl+0 to fit it on screen.~Press Ctrl+1 to inspect 100% view.~Press Ctrl+R to display rulers, then drag a guide from a ruler.~Use Hand Tool (H) to pan around the canvas and inspect the Info panel.','The document content stays fixed as the view changes and guide overlays appear.','Display transformations and editing transformations are independent.', 'Set horizontal and vertical guides at the canvas midpoint and inspect at 100%.','Guides intersect at the middle without changing any pixel.', 'Do not mistake low zoom interpolation for actual file blur.', 'At 100%, judge edge sharpness more reliably than at arbitrary zoom.', 'Does zooming to 400% add pixels?', 'No~Yes, four times~Yes, only for PSD',0)
L(1,'History, undo and disciplined experiments','Undo reverses recent actions but is not a durable backup; History states can be lost when a file closes or the list is purged. Save versions before destructive experiments.','Edit > Undo; Window > History','Create a shape layer and paint three distinct short strokes on a new pixel layer.~Press Ctrl+Z once and observe the most recent step disappear.~Open Window > History and select an earlier state.~Return to the newest desired state, then save the PSD as v02.psd.','History walks through session states; the saved PSD records only the chosen current result.','History is temporal state, not a substitute for named snapshots or file versions.','Make three editing changes, deliberately revert one and create a new saved version.','Show a v01 and v02 file and explain their differences.', 'Jumping to a past History state and making new edits may discard later redo states.', 'Duplicate a layer before testing filters or retouching.', 'What survives closing and reopening a document?', 'Saved PSD versions~All unsaved History states~Current undo buffer',0)

# Stage 2
L(2,'Canvas size, crop and image size','Canvas Size adds or removes document area; Crop changes the framing; Image Size resamples or reinterprets resolution. Their effects differ despite all changing visible bounds.','Image > Canvas Size; Crop Tool (C); Image > Image Size','Create 800 × 600 px with two Shape layers.~Choose Image > Canvas Size and set Width to 1000 px, keeping anchor centered.~Choose Crop Tool (C), drag a tighter frame and commit with Enter.~Choose Image > Image Size and compare pixel dimensions before resampling.','Canvas expands first; crop restricts framing; resampling alters sample count.', 'Three commands solve layout, composition and pixel-density problems respectively.', 'Prepare two outputs from one master: wide banner and cropped square without harming the master.', 'Record dimensions of each version and retain the original master.', 'Cropping with Delete Cropped Pixels enabled removes hidden pixels.', 'Disable Delete Cropped Pixels when you need an adjustable crop.', 'Which increases empty document area without resampling existing artwork?', 'Canvas Size~Image Size with Resample~Smart Sharpen',0)
L(2,'Create, rename, reorder and group layers','Layers composite top-to-bottom. Visibility, opacity, locks and groups change the editing structure, not the pixel size of the canvas. Accurate names matter during revisions.','Window > Layers; Layer > New > Layer; Layer > Group Layers','Create three layers with colored rectangles on each.~Rename them Background color, Middle form, and Foreground accent.~Drag their order in Layers and toggle the eye icon.~Select related layers and press Ctrl+G to create a group.','Front layers cover rear layers; hiding or reordering visibly changes overlap.','Layer order is a compositing operation, not merely a file organizer.', 'Build a three-color geometric composition using six named layers in two groups.', 'Hide either group and identify exactly what changes.', 'Painting on the wrong selected layer causes unexpected changes.', 'Use descriptive names before a file reaches dozens of layers.', 'Which panel controls layer stacking?', 'Layers~Histogram~Navigator',0)
L(2,'Move, align and distribute components','Move changes layer position; align brings selected items to a common reference; distribute spaces multiple items evenly. Transform changes dimensions, rotation or orientation.','Move Tool (V); Edit > Free Transform','Make three distinct square Shape layers.~Select all three in Layers, choose Move Tool (V), and inspect alignment/distribution controls in the Options bar.~Align their top edges, then distribute their horizontal centers.~Select one square and press Ctrl+T; resize with a corner handle and commit.','Three elements share a common top alignment and regular spacing; one element scales.', 'Geometry operations use selected layer bounds and alignment reference, not assumed eyeballing.', 'Lay out three icon tiles with consistent spacing and top alignment.', 'Use ruler guides to check alignment at 100%.','If Align is unavailable, check number of selected layers and tool settings.', 'Use guides and Smart Guides for precision, but inspect actual positions.', 'What is the purpose of Distribute?', 'Make spacing systematic~Flatten all layers~Change color profile',0)
L(2,'Transparency, alpha and the checkerboard','Transparency means pixels contribute partially or not at all to an output. The checkerboard is a display indicator, not actual artwork; exporting JPEG cannot preserve alpha.','Window > Layers; File > Export > Export As','Create a new document with Transparent Background Contents.~Draw an opaque ellipse on a Shape layer.~Hide any opaque background and observe checkerboard around it.~Use File > Export > Export As, choose PNG and enable Transparency if available.','PNG retains clear surrounding pixels; checkerboard appears only in the workspace.','An alpha channel controls opacity of pixel contributions, distinct from their RGB colors.', 'Export a 512 × 512 transparent badge with no white rectangle.', 'Open the PNG over a dark layer and verify no white border.', 'A hidden white background layer is not the same as an exported white fill.', 'Check exported transparency in a second viewer or document.', 'Which format normally preserves transparency for web assets?', 'PNG~JPEG~BMP with default settings',0)
L(2,'Layer styles and reusable appearance','Layer effects such as Stroke, Drop Shadow and Color Overlay are attached to the layer instead of baked into pixels. Effect order, size and blending affect the result.','Layer > Layer Style > Blending Options; Layers panel fx','Create a rounded rectangle Shape layer.~Double-click its empty layer row area to open Layer Style.~Add Stroke, set Position to Inside and Size to 8 px.~Add Drop Shadow with modest Distance and Size; then toggle fx visibility.','Stroke and shadow appear and can be disabled without altering the shape path.','Effects are parameterized render steps applied to content boundaries.', 'Make a reusable badge with 2 styles and save as PSD.', 'Reopen effects and change stroke width without repainting.', 'Do not confuse layer Fill 0% with Opacity 0%—styles may remain visible at Fill 0%.', 'Use subtle shadows; large opaque shadows often look artificial.', 'How can a stroke remain independently adjustable?', 'Layer Style~Rasterize layer~JPEG export',0)
L(2,'Smart Guides, grids and measurement','Guides, grids and rulers organize precision work. Snapping may be toggled; alignment must be checked rather than assumed from temporary overlays.','View > Show > Grid; View > Snap; View > Rulers','Create a 1000 × 1000 px document.~Show grid using View > Show > Grid and enable View > Snap if desired.~Draw four evenly sized shape tiles and position them near grid intersections.~Temporarily hide the grid and evaluate spacing at fit view.','Tiles align consistently without grid lines appearing in exported artwork.', 'Guides and grids are nonprinting reference overlays.', 'Construct a four-tile contact sheet with equal margins and gaps.', 'Use guides to measure equal borders on every side.', 'A grid unit is not necessarily one pixel; inspect preferences for spacing.', 'For precise print measurements, select suitable ruler units.', 'Do guides normally appear in exported PNG files?', 'No~Always~Only in CMYK',0)

# Stage 3
L(3,'Marquee and lasso: selecting regions','A selection is a temporary spatial constraint on editing, often shown as marching ants. Geometric selections are suitable for hard-edged areas; freehand selections suit irregular boundaries.','Rectangular Marquee Tool (M); Lasso Tool (L); Select > Deselect','Create a document with a colored pixel layer.~Use Rectangular Marquee (M) to select the left half.~Choose Layer > New > Layer, then Edit > Fill using a foreground color.~Press Ctrl+D, use Lasso (L) to trace a rough area and fill on a separate layer.','Only selected areas receive fills; deselection removes the boundary, not the filled pixels.', 'Selection masks specify edit coverage rather than storing artwork by themselves.', 'Create an abstract poster with one sharp rectangle and one freeform filled region.', 'Each fill occupies only its intended selected boundary.', 'Watch whether the selection is set to New, Add, Subtract or Intersect in the Options bar.', 'Feathering should be 0 px for crisp geometric artwork.', 'What does Ctrl+D do in a selection workflow?', 'Deselect~Delete selected pixels~Duplicate layer',0)
L(3,'Object Selection, Quick Selection and color range','Object Selection detects likely objects; Quick Selection grows a brush-guided selection based on edges; Color Range targets sampled colors and tolerance. Each fails differently on low contrast.','Object Selection Tool; Quick Selection Tool (W); Select > Color Range','Open the included practice/mask-shapes.png.~Use Object Selection Tool to target a distinct shape.~Switch to Quick Selection Tool and paint inside a colored region; use subtract mode to trim spill.~Choose Select > Color Range and sample a different solid color, adjusting Fuzziness; confirm with OK.','Each method creates a usable but differently shaped selection.', 'The correct selector depends on semantic objects, visible boundaries or chromatic similarity.', 'Select and isolate each of the three large colored elements using a different method.', 'After each choice, toggle Quick Mask to examine missed edges.', 'Color Range may select similar colors elsewhere in the image.', 'Use a zoomed view to validate edge quality; the AI choice is not final.', 'Which method targets repeated similar colors?', 'Color Range~Crop Tool~Character panel',0)
L(3,'Layer masks: hiding without deleting','A raster layer mask is grayscale coverage: white reveals, black hides, gray partially reveals. The pixels underneath are intact. Painting on a mask changes visibility rather than source RGB values.','Layer > Layer Mask > Reveal All; Window > Layers','Open practice/mask-shapes.png.~Duplicate the image layer with Ctrl+J.~Choose Layer > Layer Mask > Reveal All and click the mask thumbnail.~Paint black with Brush (B) to hide part of the upper layer; press X to switch colors and paint white to restore it.','Part of the duplicate disappears and returns without losing RGB pixels.', 'The mask multiplies the layer opacity at each location.', 'Create a diagonal blend between two differently colored layers using a mask.', 'You can reverse or refine the blend entirely by repainting the mask.', 'Check whether the image thumbnail or mask thumbnail is selected before painting.', 'Alt-click a mask thumbnail to inspect the grayscale mask directly.', 'What restores a hidden area on a standard white layer mask?', 'Paint white on mask~Delete pixels~Flatten document',0)
L(3,'Select and Mask: refining difficult boundaries','Edge refinement considers transitions, fine strands and color contamination. Hair and transparent items rarely have an objectively perfect binary edge. Output to a new layer with mask to preserve the original.','Select > Select and Mask','Use practice/mask-shapes.png or an owned portrait with a clear background.~Create a rough subject selection using Quick Selection.~Open Select > Select and Mask, inspect On Black and On White views.~Adjust Smooth/Feather/Contrast sparingly and Output To: New Layer with Layer Mask.','A separate subject layer appears with a retouchable mask; edge quality varies by source.', 'A refined mask models partial pixel coverage instead of hard clipping alone.', 'Extract a subject and place it on three differently colored backgrounds.', 'No obvious old-background fringe at 100% on all three tests.', 'Large Feather values cause a halo; excessive Contrast destroys wispy details.', 'Inspect both light and dark backgrounds to spot fringing.', 'Why test extraction on different backgrounds?', 'To expose edge halos~To increase file size~To improve PPI',0)
L(3,'Quick Mask, channels and saved selections','Quick Mask visualizes selection coverage as a temporary overlay. Selections can be saved as alpha channels and loaded later, independent of a layer mask. Channel thumbnails may also be inspected for contrast.','Select > Edit in Quick Mask Mode; Select > Save Selection; Window > Channels','Make a lasso selection on an image.~Press Q to enter Quick Mask and paint black or white to revise coverage.~Press Q again, then Select > Save Selection with name detail-boundary.~Deselect, open Window > Channels, and use Select > Load Selection to restore it.','The refined selected area returns after being saved and loaded.', 'An alpha channel stores grayscale selection coverage for reuse.', 'Create two named selections for separate image regions and reload each.', 'Both can be loaded without recreating the marquee.', 'Do not edit a color channel accidentally when you intend to paint the Quick Mask.', 'Name saved selections according to meaning, not date alone.', 'What does a saved alpha channel preserve?', 'Selection coverage~A complete editable font~Document history',0)
L(3,'Clipping masks versus vector masks','A clipping mask uses the transparency of a lower base layer to limit upper artwork. A vector mask uses an editable path boundary. A raster mask stores pixel coverage. They solve related but distinct problems.','Layer > Create Clipping Mask; Layer > Vector Mask','Make a text or rectangle Shape layer called container.~Place a colorful pixel layer immediately above it and choose Layer > Create Clipping Mask.~Change the base shape and watch the image clip to its outline.~On a different pixel layer, draw a path and explore Layer > Vector Mask > Current Path.','One image is bounded by base-layer opacity; another by a vector path.', 'Clipping follows the lower layer; vector masks are path-defined.', 'Place an image within a word and a separate image in an elliptical vector boundary.', 'Resizing the lower text/shape updates the visible crop.', 'A clipping mask is not the same as combining two layers permanently.', 'Use editable text or shape bases for flexible mockups.', 'What determines a clipping mask boundary?', 'Transparency of its base layer~Canvas size~Resolution metadata',0)

# Stage 4
L(4,'Adjustment layers and scoped corrections','Adjustment layers compute a tonal or color transformation above source layers. Their built-in masks let you target a region without duplicating source pixels.','Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Curves; Window > Properties','Open practice/tone-gradient.png.~Choose Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Curves.~Raise a midtone point slightly, then click the mask thumbnail and paint black over one side.~Toggle adjustment layer visibility to compare.','Only the unmasked side becomes brighter; the original gradient remains intact.', 'A function maps input tonal values to new output values, modulated by mask coverage.', 'Make the left half brighter without touching the right; save editable PSD.', 'Hiding adjustment restores the unaltered source instantly.', 'If changes affect everything, inspect the adjustment mask and clipping.', 'Name each adjustment by intent such as mids lift or cool highlights.', 'Which allows later editing of the tonal mapping?', 'Curves adjustment layer~Image > Adjustments applied to pixels~JPEG',0)
L(4,'Smart Objects: embedded versus linked','Smart Objects wrap original content so transforms and many filters can be reevaluated. Embedded content lives inside the PSD; linked content depends on an external file path. Neither guarantees lossless enlarging beyond the source detail.','File > Place Embedded; File > Place Linked; Layer > Smart Objects > Convert to Smart Object','Create a PSD master and choose File > Place Embedded for practice/mask-shapes.png.~Resize it down using Ctrl+T, commit; resize up and compare.~Place the same PNG as Linked and inspect the link indicator in Layers.~Move the linked PNG externally in Windows Explorer and note how relinking may be required.','The embedded item travels inside the PSD; linked item references an external dependency.', 'A Smart Object retains the source transform data; linked assets add file-system dependencies.', 'Make a mockup with one embedded logo and one linked reference.', 'List the external file required to reopen the project correctly.', 'Do not assume linked assets are bundled when sharing only the PSD.', 'Package assets with predictable relative folder paths.', 'Which is self-contained within the PSD?', 'Embedded Smart Object~Linked Smart Object~Missing link',0)
L(4,'Smart Filters and reversible blur','Filters applied to supported Smart Objects appear as editable Smart Filters with their own mask. Some filters and document modes impose restrictions; not all operations become Smart Filters.','Layer > Smart Objects > Convert to Smart Object; Filter > Blur > Gaussian Blur','Create a detailed pixel layer and convert it to Smart Object.~Choose Filter > Blur > Gaussian Blur and set Radius to 6 px.~Double-click Gaussian Blur under Smart Filters and change Radius to 2 px.~Paint black on the Smart Filters mask over a detail that must remain sharp.','The blur changes reversibly; selected detail escapes the filter.', 'The filter parameters are stored separately from source pixels and masked after computation.', 'Blur a simple background while keeping a central badge sharp.', 'Change blur radius twice after saving without duplicating source pixels.', 'A large blur mask painted white exposes blur, not underlying image visibility.', 'Place filters on Smart Objects before committing a design.', 'Which reopens Gaussian Blur parameters?', 'Double-click its Smart Filter entry~Rasterize~Save a JPEG',0)
L(4,'Blend modes, opacity and fill','Blend modes mathematically combine layer pixel values with the composite below. Opacity scales the entire layer contribution, while Fill changes the interior contribution and can behave differently with layer styles.','Window > Layers > blend mode / Opacity / Fill','Make a gray-filled layer above colored rectangles.~Test Multiply, Screen and Overlay, observing darkening/lightening tendencies.~Add a Stroke layer style to the top layer.~Lower Fill to 0%, then restore and lower Opacity to 0% to compare the style.','Multiply generally darkens, Screen brightens; Fill and Opacity affect styles differently.', 'Blend functions and alpha multiplication are distinct steps in rendering.', 'Create three variations of a textured poster with different blending modes.', 'Explain the visual difference rather than choosing a mode by name alone.', 'Blending depends strongly on the colors below; no mode has a universal look.', 'Compare at 100% and in the intended output color space.', 'Which setting can hide fill while leaving some layer effects visible?', 'Fill 0%~Opacity 0%~Lock Pixels',0)
L(4,'Blend If and conditional compositing','Blend If uses underlying or current layer brightness to reveal and conceal areas without painting masks. Split slider halves make transitions gradual; tonal changes below can alter the blend.','Layer > Layer Style > Blending Options','Create a grayscale gradient layer and a bright color texture above it.~Open Blending Options for the upper layer.~Under Blend If: Gray, move the Underlying Layer dark slider to reveal dark background areas.~Alt-drag one half of a slider to split its ramp and smooth the transition.','The upper texture fades selectively based on underlying brightness.', 'Luminance-conditioned blending is an automatic mask derived from pixel intensities.', 'Integrate a light streak into a dark gradient without erasing pixels.', 'The transition remains editable and shows no abrupt hard band.', 'A non-split slider can create severe clipping edges.', 'Split sliders while zoomed in to evaluate haloes.', 'What does Underlying Layer control?', 'Reveal based on lower composite tone~Selection size~Document PPI',0)
L(4,'Versionable editing and review handoff','Professional files should record structure, dependencies, revision status and flattened previews. Nondestructive editing is also about naming, file integrity and a reliable recovery route.','File > Save As; File > Save a Copy; Window > Layers','Open an earlier project and group source, adjustments and design layers.~Name groups 01_SOURCE, 02_EDIT and 03_OUTPUT.~Save a new PSD revision and export a PNG preview.~Close and reopen the revision, confirm no missing linked elements or font warnings.','Editable master reopens with organized, visible layers and preview matches the intended version.', 'Stable naming and verification prevent lost context between sessions.', 'Deliver a revisioned PSD and PNG preview with a short change note.', 'Another editor can find the source and isolate your final changes.', 'A preview is not a substitute for the master.', 'Keep the source layer untouched or backed up.', 'What is best for an editable client revision?', 'Organized PSD plus preview~Single flattened PNG~Unlabeled working history',0)

# Stage 5
L(5,'Spot healing versus healing brush','Spot Healing estimates replacement texture automatically; Healing Brush samples from a chosen source and adapts tonal appearance. Both are preferable on a separate cleanup layer when tool settings permit.','Spot Healing Brush Tool (J); Healing Brush Tool (J)','Open practice/retouch-dots.png and add an empty layer named cleanup.~Choose Spot Healing Brush, set Sample All Layers in Options (if available), and dab individual dots.~Switch to Healing Brush; Alt-click a clean sample near a larger defect, then paint it.~Toggle cleanup visibility and compare texture.','Small colored defects disappear while surrounding gradient texture remains continuous.', 'Healing uses local statistics and source texture to blend a correction.', 'Remove 10 intentionally planted spots without painting on the source layer.', 'At 100%, repair boundaries are not visibly smeared.', 'Oversized brush tips create soft or duplicated details; sample nearby.', 'Work from small blemishes to larger regions and inspect at 100%.', 'Why use a blank cleanup layer?', 'To preserve original pixels~To make JPEG lossless~To increase PPI',0)
L(5,'Clone Stamp, Patch and pattern repetition','Clone Stamp copies sampled pixels, including both texture and tonal pattern; Patch repairs a selected area using another region and may blend boundary behavior. Clone is precise but repeating source patterns can betray edits.','Clone Stamp Tool (S); Patch Tool (J)','Open practice/retouch-dots.png and duplicate the background.~Use Clone Stamp (S); Alt-click a clean region and paint over an obvious spot.~Use the Patch Tool to outline a separate blemish and drag to clean texture (Source mode).~Inspect for repeated motifs; undo any noticeable repetition.','Damage is replaced, but careless cloning can reproduce exact nearby shapes.', 'Sampling is literal reconstruction rather than generation from scene semantics.', 'Repair a linear blemish using multiple short source samples.', 'No recognizable repeated dot or mirrored texture in the repaired region.', 'Do not sample across a strong lighting gradient unnecessarily.', 'Resample frequently and work on an empty layer when Clone Stamp sampling supports it.', 'What is the main risk of repeated cloning from one spot?', 'Visible repeated pattern~Loss of ruler units~Missing alpha channel',0)
L(5,'Remove tool and content-aware corrections','Remove Tool estimates plausible replacement content from surrounding context; Content-Aware Fill gives more explicit region and sampling control. Some Remove models process on device after download; cloud options differ.','Remove Tool (J group); Edit > Content-Aware Fill','Open a photograph or the retouch practice PNG; duplicate or create a cleanup layer.~Paint over an unwanted mark using Remove Tool, using its configured processing mode.~Undo if needed; select a larger unwanted area with Lasso.~Choose Edit > Content-Aware Fill, inspect preview/sampling region and output to a new layer.','Unwanted area is reconstructed; results differ by local texture and model choice.', 'These methods infer missing content and may invent implausible structure.', 'Remove an isolated mark and a larger distraction, compare both methods.', 'Edges, repeated lines and texture remain consistent at 100%.', 'Generated repairs must be checked for duplicated objects and factual distortion.', 'For flat surfaces, traditional healing may be faster and more reproducible.', 'Which gives explicit sampling-region preview?', 'Content-Aware Fill workspace~Marquee tool~History panel',0)
L(5,'Portrait retouching: texture and restraint','Retouching should preserve pore texture, facial shape and identity. Separate tonal unevenness from isolated texture blemishes; global skin blurring loses realistic detail.','Healing Brush Tool; Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Curves','Use an authorized portrait; duplicate the source layer and create cleanup above it.~Heal isolated temporary blemishes at 100%, not permanent identifying marks by default.~Add a Curves adjustment layer and invert its mask using Ctrl+I after selecting the mask.~Paint soft white at low brush opacity to lighten only selected shadow regions.','Skin looks naturally even, with fine texture intact and no obvious patches.', 'Selective local correction changes specific problems while preserving normal surface variation.', 'Edit a portrait using no more than two healing passes and two subtle tonal adjustments.', 'Toggle off all retouch layers: identity and natural skin texture remain recognizable.', 'Avoid excessive smoothing, reshaping or removing meaningful identity features.', 'Zoom out regularly to judge whether local edits remain believable.', 'Best first step for one small blemish?', 'Localized healing~Global blur~Flatten all layers',0)
L(5,'Dodge and burn for form','Dodge brightens and burn darkens local areas. Separate Curves layers with masks provide adjustable and often safer light shaping than repeatedly brushing the original pixels.','Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Curves; Brush Tool (B)','Create a curves layer raising midtones and name Dodge.~Invert its white mask to black with Ctrl+I.~Create a second Curves layer lowering midtones named Burn; invert mask.~Paint white at low opacity on respective masks to refine highlights and shadows.','Local form and contrast improve without obvious bright or dark brush circles.', 'Small spatial tone changes reinforce perceived lighting and shape.', 'Build a simple ball from practice/tone-gradient.png using subtle dodge/burn.', 'Smooth gradual change with no patchy halos at 100%.', 'Painting a mask at 100% brush opacity creates harsh patches.', 'Use a soft brush at 5–15% opacity for gradual work.', 'Where should you paint for editable dodge/burn?', 'On Curves layer masks~Directly on background pixels~In Channels as RGB',0)
L(5,'Liquify, perspective and ethical changes','Liquify warps local geometry; perspective correction aligns planar structures. Geometric editing can alter identity and meaning: disclose material manipulations in documentary or evidentiary contexts.','Filter > Liquify; Edit > Transform > Perspective','Create a patterned layer and convert to a Smart Object.~Choose Filter > Liquify, use Forward Warp with modest pressure and commit.~Reopen the Smart Filter to revise; compare straight lines.~Separately use Edit > Transform > Perspective on a shape and inspect convergence.','Objects bend or converge predictably; Smart Filter settings remain reversible when supported.', 'Warping moves sample coordinates and can distort backgrounds and scale cues.', 'Correct an obviously tilted rectangular label while preserving its proportions.', 'Edges align more plausibly without stretching the text unnaturally.', 'Do not use Liquify casually for identity-sensitive retouching.', 'Use a duplicate/Smart Object and compare to unedited reference.', 'What makes Liquify adjustable after application?', 'Applying as supported Smart Filter~Flattening image~JPEG save',0)
L(5,'Old photo repair and reconstruction','Restoration begins with separating tone/fading, dirt/scratches and missing geometry. Reconstructing missing faces or text is inherently interpretive, not an authentic recovery of historical facts.','Spot Healing Brush; Clone Stamp Tool; Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Levels','Open an owned scanned photograph or practice/retouch-dots.png.~Duplicate the image layer and crop only outside borders if permitted.~Use Levels adjustment to set sensible tonal endpoints without clipping.~Retouch scratches on a separate layer; rebuild repeated lines with short Clone Stamp samples.','Contrast improves and major marks recede while original structure stays preserved.', 'Restoration is constrained inference: maintain provenance and reversible corrections.', 'Produce before/after and a note describing which pixels were reconstructed.', 'Original scan remains retained; reconstruction is documented.', 'Do not treat AI-invented missing historical details as recovered truth.', 'Keep a separate original archival scan with embedded profile.', 'For archival restoration, which is mandatory?', 'Retain original and document changes~Overwrite scan~Delete history of intervention',0)

# Stage 6
L(6,'Brush fundamentals: tip, size and hardness','Brush Tip Shape defines diameter and hardness, Opacity controls stroke contribution, and Flow controls accumulation as the tip moves. Low flow builds color gradually.','Brush Tool (B); Window > Brush Settings','Create a transparent layer and choose Brush Tool (B).~Paint three strokes at Hardness 100%, 50%, and 0% using the same diameter.~Set Opacity to 30% and paint a single stroke; then set Flow to 10% and make overlapping strokes.~Compare the buildup at intersections and edges.','Hard brushes have crisp edges; soft brushes feather; low-flow strokes accumulate color.', 'A brush deposits samples along a path, blended with existing pixels.', 'Create a reference swatch of 4 brush behaviors, label it with Type.', 'Describe which produces crisp contours and which builds shading.', 'Painting directly on the white Background loses flexibility.', 'Use [ and ] for brush diameter only when the brush is focused and shortcuts are default.', 'What makes a stroke build slowly with repeated passes?', 'Low Flow~Higher PPI~Canvas Size',0)
L(6,'Brush dynamics and tablet pressure','Shape Dynamics may vary diameter with pen pressure or other inputs. Without a pressure-capable tablet, pressure controls do not respond as expected. Smoothing stabilizes trajectories at a cost in immediacy.','Window > Brush Settings; Brush Tool Options bar','Open Brush Settings and enable Shape Dynamics.~Inspect Size Jitter Control and choose Pen Pressure only if a pressure tablet is available.~Paint slow and fast curves with Smoothing 0% and then a higher value.~Compare curves; if no tablet exists, use a fixed tip and note the limitation.','Stroke taper depends on tablet pressure; smoothing reduces small jagged deviations.', 'Input devices determine which brush dynamics are measurable.', 'Make a labeled brush study with fixed size, scatter, and smoothing examples.', 'Report whether pen pressure is available and demonstrate smooth deliberate strokes.', 'Do not mistake absent tablet pressure for an application defect.', 'Save a tested brush preset with a descriptive name.', 'What hardware enables true pen-pressure size control?', 'Pressure-sensitive tablet~Ordinary mouse only~External SSD',0)
L(6,'Color picker, swatches and sampling','Foreground/Background colors are active state, distinct from color profiles. Eyedropper samples pixels or composites; Swatches store reusable color choices.','Foreground color swatch; Eyedropper Tool (I); Window > Swatches','Open practice/color-palette.png.~Select Eyedropper (I), click a palette square and inspect Foreground color.~Open Color Picker and record hexadecimal RGB value.~Create a new layer and paint two sampled colors; save them as Swatches if supported.','Brush strokes match sampled palette tones when same color-management conditions apply.', 'Sampling records channel values of rendered pixels for later reuse.', 'Create a 5-color palette strip and verify hex values for each.', 'Colors can be independently repainted from saved swatches.', 'Eyedropper sample size and Sample settings can alter noisy-edge results.', 'Sample flat interior areas, not anti-aliased edges.', 'Which control samples a pixel color?', 'Eyedropper~Hand~Dodge',0)
L(6,'Gradients, patterns and fill layers','A Gradient Fill layer generates a controllable continuous transition; Pattern Fill tiles a source. Compared with painting directly, fill layers preserve their parameters and mask options.','Layer > New Fill Layer > Gradient; Layer > New Fill Layer > Pattern','Create a 1000 × 600 px document.~Choose Layer > New Fill Layer > Gradient; select linear transition and adjust Angle.~Open Gradient Fill properties later and change Scale.~Add a Pattern Fill layer if patterns are installed; adjust Scale then hide it to compare.','Background colors form a consistent gradual sweep; pattern repeat changes with scale.', 'Fill layers render procedural pixels during compositing.', 'Produce three backgrounds from the same master using adjustable fills.', 'No hand-painted seam or uneven banding visible at usual output zoom.', 'Very narrow gradients in 8-bit images may band visibly.', 'Consider 16-bit mode for demanding smooth gradients, subject to export support.', 'What remains editable on a Gradient Fill layer?', 'Gradient and angle~Only history~JPEG quantization',0)
L(6,'Eraser, history brush and restoration methods','Eraser removes or reduces pixels; a mask usually provides the same visual result more safely. History Brush can paint an earlier image state into current pixels but depends on History source availability.','Eraser Tool (E); History Brush Tool (Y); Window > History','Make a pixel layer with colored paint and duplicate it.~Erase part of the duplicate with Eraser (E) and compare with a mask on another copy.~Open History panel and review source states for History Brush.~Save and reopen: observe that the original History sequence does not remain available.','Mask-based hiding is reversible; erasing changes alpha of the chosen pixel layer.', 'Editing coverage and removal of stored pixels are separate concepts.', 'Recreate the same missing area using Eraser and using a layer mask.', 'Only the masked version can restore hidden content by painting white.', 'Do not assume History Brush can resurrect changes after closing Photoshop.', 'Default to masks unless permanent pixel deletion is intentional.', 'Which hides pixels without deleting their RGB content?', 'Layer mask~Eraser on raster layer~Flatten Image',0)
L(6,'Digital painting with layers and value structure','Readable digital painting begins with large value masses, then color and edge hierarchy, and finally detail. Layer organization prevents foreground/background mixing.','Brush Tool (B); Window > Layers','Set up a 1200 × 800 px RGB canvas with background, midground and foreground layers.~Paint three large value masses in grayscale using a broad soft brush.~Add smaller hard-edged focal forms, then introduce a Color blend mode layer for hue.~Check the design in grayscale using a temporary Black & White adjustment.','A coherent scene reads by value even before small details appear.', 'Perception of form depends more on large contrasts than local decorative detail.', 'Paint a simple cityscape with a clear focal point using at most five layers.', 'At thumbnail size the focal object is still identifiable.', 'Adding small details before solving composition wastes time.', 'Toggle between fit view and 100% while painting.', 'What should be established first in a painting?', 'Large value structure~Individual eyelashes~Output ZIP name',0)

# Stage 7
L(7,'Point type, paragraph type and editable text','Point type is created by clicking; paragraph type uses a bounding box. Type stays editable until rasterized, and font substitution can change line breaks and layout.','Horizontal Type Tool (T); Window > Character; Window > Paragraph','Create a 1200 × 800 px document.~Choose Type Tool (T), click once and type a short headline.~Drag a text box and paste three sentences for paragraph type.~Change font size and leading in Character panel, and compare wrapping before and after resizing box.','Headline remains a single type object; paragraph text wraps within its frame.', 'Type layers store characters and layout rules rather than fixed painted shapes.', 'Design a typographic title plus a two-column paragraph block.', 'Both remain editable; text does not overflow its intended boxes.', 'Selecting the Move Tool while editing text may require committing the text first.', 'Keep critical fonts installed and package font licensing information appropriately.', 'What makes a paragraph box wrap text?', 'Its bounding width~Image PPI~Layer opacity',0)
L(7,'Kerning, tracking, leading and hierarchy','Kerning changes a specific character pair; tracking adjusts spacing across a selection; leading sets baseline-to-baseline distance. Good hierarchy depends on consistent spacing, size and contrast.','Window > Character; Window > Paragraph','Type AVATAR as a headline and select the AV pair.~In Character panel, compare Metrics/Optical kerning where available and manual pair adjustment.~Select the entire heading and adjust Tracking.~Create three body lines and change Leading to compare dense versus airy layout.','Letter and line spacing visibly change without changing the wording.', 'Typography works as a spatial rhythm, not only the choice of font.', 'Create three text levels: title, subheading and body; align to a common edge.', 'Hierarchy is obvious in 3 seconds and no line collisions occur.', 'Overtracking small body text damages legibility.', 'Audit at actual output size, not just an enlarged canvas.', 'Which sets spacing between text baselines?', 'Leading~Kerning~Opacity',0)
L(7,'Shape tools and vector operations','Shape layers contain paths with fills and strokes. Combining, subtracting and intersecting shapes controls topology while keeping crisp edges at working resolution.','Rectangle Tool (U); Shape mode; Path Operations in Options bar','Choose Rectangle Tool (U) and set tool mode to Shape.~Draw one rectangle and then an ellipse with separate Shape layers.~Select one Shape layer and use Path Operations to subtract an inner hole where supported.~Change fill, stroke and corner radius in Properties.','The composite geometry responds to path operations and stays editable.', 'Shape paths define coverage mathematically, with fills and strokes rendered at export.', 'Create a simple icon with a hole and 2 shape colors.', 'Icon still has crisp boundaries after a reversible transform.', 'Drawing in Pixels mode rasterizes output instead of adding editable shapes.', 'Check mode (Shape/Path/Pixels) before drawing.', 'Which mode produces a vector-edged editable shape layer?', 'Shape~Pixels~Quick Mask',0)
L(7,'Pen tool, anchor points and Bézier handles','A Bézier path consists of anchors and direction handles; moving handles changes tangent direction and curvature. Fewer well-placed anchors typically produce smoother curves.','Pen Tool (P); Direct Selection Tool (A); Window > Paths','Select Pen Tool (P) and choose Path mode.~Click two points for a straight segment; click-drag the third to create smooth handles.~Close the contour, then select Direct Selection (A) and modify a handle.~Convert path to selection using Paths panel; create a masked fill from it.','Curved boundary changes as handles move while original samples remain untouched.', 'Bezier segments interpolate a smooth curve between control points.', 'Trace a simple leaf outline with six or fewer anchors.', 'Curve has no visible kinks where the contour should be smooth.', 'Too many points create lumpy geometry and make revision slow.', 'Place anchors at major direction changes, not every few pixels.', 'What primarily controls Bézier curvature?', 'Direction handles~PPI~Blend mode',0)
L(7,'Text on paths and shape-following type','Text on a path remains editable and its baseline follows the vector path. Dynamic Text features available in recent stable releases can reflow certain shapes, but are not prerequisites for standard path text.','Pen Tool (P); Type Tool (T)','Draw a curved path in Path mode with the Pen Tool.~Choose Type Tool and hover until the insertion cursor indicates path text.~Click the path and type a short phrase; use path selection to reposition the text start/end controls.~Adjust tracking to fit the curve without compression.','Words follow the curve rather than a horizontal box.', 'Glyph positions are laid out along a geometric baseline.', 'Create a circular seal with a curved top caption and a straight subtitle.', 'Type remains editable and avoids overlap.', 'Crowded path text becomes unreadable; choose shorter copy.', 'Use ordinary path text before exploring 27.10 Dynamic Text behavior.', 'What does path text follow?', 'A vector baseline~A raster mask~History states',0)
L(7,'Layout system and reusable mockup','Reusable graphics use guides, named layers, Smart Objects and text styles. A mockup should separate replaceable content from lighting, surface detail and branding.','View > New Guide; File > Place Embedded; Window > Layers','Create a 1600 × 900 px document and guides for 64 px margins.~Draw a device/screen placeholder with a rectangular Shape layer.~Place the included color-palette PNG as an embedded Smart Object; position it over the screen.~Add editable heading, subtitle and call-to-action text in groups.','Layout remains aligned and each asset is replaceable without flattening.', 'Modularity keeps content swaps separate from permanent scene design.', 'Swap the placed image with a different PNG while preserving headline placement.', 'No overlapping text, inconsistent margins, or misplaced layers.', 'A stretched placeholder can distort imported art; preserve aspect ratio.', 'Use consistent margin spacing as a simple design system.', 'What makes a mockup reusable?', 'Separate editable content and structure~One flattened image~Repeated destructive crops',0)

# Stage 8
L(8,'Histogram, exposure and tonal clipping','Histogram graphs the number of pixels at tonal values. It reveals distribution and possible clipping but does not declare a single correct exposure; a night scene may naturally cluster left.','Window > Histogram; Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Levels','Open practice/tone-gradient.png and show Window > Histogram.~Add a Levels adjustment layer.~Move black and white point sliders inward slowly, watching endpoints; then reset and move midtone slider.~Observe whether fine gradient steps lose distinction near the extremes.','Distribution and perceived contrast change; extreme endpoint changes clip detail.', 'Levels remaps tonal values using input black, gamma-like midtone and white controls.', 'Make two versions: gentle contrast and intentionally clipped contrast.', 'Explain which image retained more steps in bright and dark regions.', 'Never force every histogram to span full width by habit.', 'Use clipping preview methods when available and inspect at 100%.', 'What does a histogram primarily show?', 'Tonal frequency distribution~Exact color truth~Printer resolution',0)
L(8,'Curves and channel-level color balance','Curves independently remaps each channel, enabling contrast and color correction. Multiple anchored points let you preserve some values while changing others; aggressive curves can posterize smooth tones.','Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Curves; Window > Properties','Open a colorful practice image and add Curves adjustment.~Place two anchors around quarter and three-quarter tones, build a gentle S curve.~Choose Red channel and lift the midtone slightly; observe warmth shift.~Undo color move, then compare composite RGB and single-channel results.','S curve boosts perceived midtone contrast; red-channel lift warms affected regions.', 'Per-channel mappings alter color balance because RGB outputs no longer change identically.', 'Fix a deliberately cool image with an adjustable channel curve.', 'Neutral grays remain nearly neutral while overall color becomes more plausible.', 'Large single-channel edits may tint intended neutrals.', 'Use multiple anchor points to protect highlights and shadows.', 'Which Curves channel changes red without directly modifying green mapping?', 'Red~RGB composite~Luminosity only',0)
L(8,'White balance, HSL and selective grading','White balance neutralizes lighting tint; HSL adjustments target hue families, saturation and luminance. Color grading is a deliberate creative look layered on a technically credible base.','Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Color Balance; Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Hue/Saturation','Open practice/color-palette.png as a reference for stable colors.~Add Color Balance adjustment and inspect Midtones Cyan/Red changes; reset afterward.~Add Hue/Saturation adjustment; choose a named color range instead of Master.~Move Hue modestly and observe which swatches react.','Selected color families change while some other colors remain comparatively steady.', 'Channel weighting and hue classification are different control models.', 'Build a natural and a stylized grade using separate adjustment groups.', 'Both can be toggled independently without source loss.', 'Strong saturation increases can clip output gamut and skin tones.', 'Use masks for global adjustments that contaminate unrelated areas.', 'Which adjusts one color family without shifting all hues equally?', 'Targeted Hue/Saturation range~Image Size~Crop',0)
L(8,'RAW development and Camera Raw Smart Objects','Camera Raw interprets sensor data and stores edits non-destructively; JPEG and TIFF can enter its tools but cannot recover data already discarded by camera processing. Open as Smart Object to preserve reopenable raw controls.','File > Open for raw; Filter > Camera Raw Filter; Help > About Plug-In > Camera Raw','Open a supported owned camera RAW file via File > Open; review white balance and exposure in Camera Raw.~Hold Shift when the Open Image button offers Open Object (label varies by context) or use Camera Raw workflow options to open as Smart Object.~Double-click the Smart Object thumbnail in Layers to reopen Camera Raw settings.~For a non-RAW image converted to Smart Object, compare Filter > Camera Raw Filter.','Raw Smart Object retains revisable develop settings; ordinary filter works on rendered pixels.', 'RAW processing starts with sensor measurements, while Camera Raw Filter receives an already-rendered image.', 'Develop a RAW landscape in two separate mood versions.', 'Reopen a saved Smart Object and adjust white balance without re-importing.', 'Camera RAW is not the same as Photoshop Raw file format.', 'Check supported camera models and Camera Raw plug-in version for new cameras.', 'What is preserved by a RAW Smart Object?', 'Reopenable RAW development settings~Infinite sensor detail~An unchanged JPEG export',0)
L(8,'RGB, CMYK, Lab, grayscale and bit depth','Color mode defines channels; profiles interpret numbers; bit depth controls channel precision. RGB generally suits photo/screen editing, CMYK depends on print process, Lab separates perceptual lightness/chroma axes.','Image > Mode; Edit > Convert to Profile; Edit > Color Settings','Duplicate a colorful PSD and inspect Image > Mode > RGB Color and 8 Bits/Channel.~Open Edit > Convert to Profile; inspect available destination spaces without committing at first.~Make a copy and try Image > Mode > Grayscale, noting the loss of color info.~Keep original RGB master untouched for comparison.','Grayscale copy loses chromatic information; profile conversion affects numerical representation.', 'The same numbers appear differently under different color interpretations.', 'Produce two named variants for display and grayscale proofing.', 'Original RGB file opens unchanged; profile choices documented.', 'Assign Profile and Convert to Profile are not interchangeable.', 'Obtain printer-specific ICC guidance before committing to CMYK conversion.', 'Which action preserves appearance better across profiles when performed correctly?', 'Convert to Profile~Blindly assign unrelated profile~Delete ICC metadata',0)
L(8,'Soft proofing, gamut and print preparation','Soft proofing simulates an output profile on your monitor; gamut warnings are approximations, not a substitute for a calibrated display and printer proof. Print success depends on PPI, profile, paper and output sharpening.','View > Proof Setup > Custom; View > Proof Colors; File > Print','Open a finished RGB image and make a duplicate.~Choose View > Proof Setup > Custom and select a real printer/paper ICC if supplied; otherwise use an available reference profile and label it a simulation.~Toggle View > Proof Colors.~Inspect Image Size print dimensions with Resample disabled and confirm document bleed requirements externally.','Soft-proof appearance may change, but source pixels are not converted merely by toggling proof.', 'Display color management models expected output gamut and tone behavior.', 'Make an annotated proofing checklist for a 6 × 4 inch print at 300 PPI.', 'Target output has at least 1800 × 1200 pixels without invented detail.', 'Do not promise precise print color without paper, printer, ink and profile details.', 'Preserve the tagged RGB master and supply printer-requested format separately.', 'Does View > Proof Colors permanently convert image pixels?', 'No~Yes~Only in PSD',0)
L(8,'Sharpening, noise and HDR discipline','Sharpening boosts local contrast; it cannot truly reconstruct lost focus. Noise reduction trades grain against small detail. HDR 32-bit workflows have display and export restrictions, so test final targets.','Filter > Sharpen > Smart Sharpen; Filter > Noise > Reduce Noise; Image > Mode','Duplicate a detailed photo or use practice/retouch-dots.png.~Convert duplicate to Smart Object; apply Filter > Sharpen > Smart Sharpen with a small Amount and Radius.~At 100%, compare halos around high-contrast lines; adjust downward.~Try a noise-reduction filter on a separate Smart Object and compare fine detail.','Moderate sharpening clarifies edges; too much creates light/dark halos and artificial texture.', 'Sharpening and denoising are competing spatial-frequency operations.', 'Create a before/after crop for web versus print sharpness.', 'Edges are enhanced but no visible bright halos or plastic texture appear.', 'Never judge sharpening solely at Fit on Screen.', 'Do output sharpening only after final pixel dimensions are fixed.', 'What does oversharpening commonly produce?', 'Bright/dark edge halos~Extra sensor information~Larger gamut',0)

# Stage 9
L(9,'Composite planning: light, camera and scale','A convincing composite must agree in horizon, perspective, relative scale, direction of light, sharpness, noise and color. Solving camera geometry first avoids wasting time on effects.','File > Place Embedded; Edit > Free Transform; View > Rulers','Create a background with a visible floor/horizon from simple shape layers.~Place an isolated PNG shape as a Smart Object.~Align its apparent base with the floor plane and scale it relative to a known reference.~Draw arrows on a separate planning layer showing camera horizon and light direction.','Placed subject occupies a plausible plane with declared light and size assumptions.', 'Visual plausibility emerges from consistency across several independent cues.', 'Assemble a three-object scene and write a one-sentence lighting plan.', 'All objects share coherent horizon and light direction.', 'Matching colors cannot fix impossible perspective.', 'Plan in grayscale before refining saturation.', 'What should be solved before elaborate grading?', 'Perspective and scale~Decorative texture~Export compression',0)
L(9,'Realistic contact and cast shadows','Contact shadows anchor objects where they touch a plane; cast shadows project away from a light source and typically soften with distance. A single black Gaussian Blur shadow rarely fits every scene.','Layer > New > Layer; Edit > Transform; Filter > Blur > Gaussian Blur','Place an isolated object above a light floor layer.~Create a new shadow layer beneath it and paint a tight dark contact shape.~Draw a longer low-opacity cast silhouette, transform it along floor direction.~Convert shadow layer to Smart Object, blur slightly and reduce opacity.','Object feels attached to the plane; shadow direction follows planned light.', 'Light occlusion reduces illumination in geometrically consistent regions.', 'Produce two versions of same scene with different light directions.', 'Each shadow direction and softness matches its chosen light.', 'Hard contact edges and distant shadow softness should not be identical.', 'Use separate contact and cast shadow layers for control.', 'Which shadow anchors an object at its base?', 'Contact shadow~Lens flare~Outer Glow',0)
L(9,'Edge integration and local color matching','Edge fringing, chromatic mismatch and inappropriate focus announce a cutout. Use subject masks, Curves, local color adjustments and selective blur/grain instead of one global filter.','Select > Select and Mask; Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Curves','Place an isolated object above a contrasting background.~Inspect cutout at 100% on both dark and light backgrounds.~Refine layer mask edge, then clip a Curves adjustment to subject.~Match subject black/white points to scene and add subtle edge softening only where needed.','Subject no longer carries a conspicuous fringe; tonal range aligns with background.', 'Compositing succeeds when each edge reproduces the background transition expected by the viewer.', 'Insert a new cutout into two different scenes and refine each separately.', 'No obvious halo, incorrect sharpness or color boundary at actual viewing size.', 'Global blur to hide a bad mask destroys subject detail.', 'Evaluate cutouts at both 100% and final output zoom.', 'What is the most targeted way to fix subject-only tone?', 'Clipped Curves adjustment~Global Exposure adjustment~Change canvas size',0)
L(9,'Depth, atmosphere and selective focus','Distance tends to reduce contrast and sometimes shift hue toward atmospheric light; depth of field depends on camera focus and aperture. Apply effects by depth groups instead of blanket fog and blur.','Layer masks; Filter > Blur > Gaussian Blur','Make a three-plane scene with foreground, midground and background shapes.~Convert background to Smart Object and apply mild Gaussian Blur.~Add a pale atmosphere layer, mask it to reveal mostly distant objects.~Compare layers with and without reductions in distant contrast.','Far objects appear less contrasted and softer, while foreground retains detail.', 'Scattering and focus cues help indicate distance.', 'Build three distinct depth bands and caption which cues create separation.', 'Foreground and distance read as different planes even at thumbnail size.', 'Uniform blur makes the entire scene look out of focus.', 'Vary effect strength by depth masks and light sources.', 'Which plane usually receives the strongest atmospheric haze?', 'Far background~Near focal subject~All equally',0)
L(9,'Displacement and realistic surface mockups','Displacement maps perturb coordinates using tonal data from a surface map; they help graphics inherit material folds or curvature. They do not automatically reproduce realistic surface shading.','Filter > Distort > Displace; File > Save As','Create a grayscale texture and save it locally as a PSD displacement map.~Place a logo as a Smart Object over a textured base image.~Use Filter > Distort > Displace on a suitable raster or Smart Object layer, choose modest horizontal/vertical scale and select the map PSD.~Try Multiply or luminosity blending and mask unwanted areas.','Logo geometry follows some surface undulations but remains visually readable.', 'A map converts grayscale changes to positional offsets; shading must still be integrated.', 'Mock a graphic onto a wrinkled material and retain original flat logo.', 'The logo follows folds without severe tears or inconsistent shadows.', 'Exaggerated displacement creates warped lettering and obvious artifact.', 'Start with small displacement values and keep the map at appropriate dimensions.', 'What does a displacement map encode?', 'Geometric pixel offsets~Font licenses~Printer profile',0)
L(9,'Frequency separation and advanced retouch judgement','Frequency separation places broad color/tone and fine texture on different layers. It can be powerful but introduces halos, plastic skin and workflow overhead if used automatically. Start with local healing and dodge/burn instead.','Filter > Blur > Gaussian Blur; Image > Apply Image (advanced)','Duplicate an owned test image twice and name layers low frequency and high frequency.~Blur the low frequency layer only enough to reduce fine texture.~Study Image > Apply Image options and channel/bit-depth dependence before computing high frequency; avoid assuming one formula fits every document.~Compare with an ordinary healing/dodge-burn edit and keep the result that retains texture.','A properly constructed pair can reproduce the source when combined; careless settings change tone.', 'Spatial frequencies describe detail scale, not separate physical layers in the photographed subject.', 'Document a before/after explaining why frequency separation was or was not necessary.', 'No plastic texture, halos or color bleeding around boundaries.', 'Do not use unverified one-click Apply Image formulas across 8/16/32-bit modes.', 'Begin with a reversible duplicate and validate reconstruction before retouching.', 'Is frequency separation always the preferred portrait workflow?', 'No~Yes~Only for CMYK',0)
L(9,'Blend layers into a high-quality final composite','Final compositing integrates shadows, local grading, common grain, edge quality, proofing and delivery. A unified adjustment can help, but it must not conceal unresolved geometry.','Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Curves; File > Export > Export As','Open a composite with at least three independent layer groups.~Inspect perspective and masks at 100% before final grading.~Create a restrained top-level Curves grade, optionally add controlled texture on a separate layer.~Export PNG/JPEG and compare at final viewing size with the PSD version.','Exported scene looks coherent at both actual pixels and reduced size.', 'Final cohesion combines local corrections with global rendering consistency.', 'Deliver an 1800 × 1200 scene with editable object groups and a flattened proof.', 'No obvious clipped mask, unsupported shadow, missing source or accidental crop.', 'A global color overlay cannot repair incorrect edges or scale.', 'Use a final visual checklist and preserve source groups.', 'Which is the final quality standard?', 'Cohesion at actual output size~Maximum filter count~Smallest PSD possible',0)

# Stage 10
L(10,'Folder structure, asset naming and linked delivery','A professional source package must retain original assets, rights notes, working PSDs, exports, and versioned revisions. File dependencies should survive a transfer to another Windows machine.','File > Place Linked; File > Save As','Create folders source, work, exports and references in Windows File Explorer.~Place a linked local PNG from source into a new PSD.~Save PSD into work and export a PNG into exports.~Copy the entire project folder to a new location and open the PSD from there; relink if required.','PSD opens with complete visual content and exports are clearly separated.', 'An editable document is only portable if dependencies and metadata travel with it.', 'Prepare a folder that another editor can inspect without a verbal explanation.', 'Every linked file exists and file names indicate purpose.', 'Absolute or changed link paths can break when folders move.', 'Bundle linked source files and avoid arbitrary names like final_FINAL2.', 'Where should immutable original inputs live?', 'source folder~exports folder only~temporary cache',0)
L(10,'Actions and batch processing','Actions record repeatable UI operations; Batch applies them to many files. Their reliability depends on dialogs, file assumptions and target naming. Start with a small test set.','Window > Actions; File > Automate > Batch','Make three copies of practice/color-palette.png in a temporary folder.~Open Window > Actions, create an action and record a safe resize or adjustment on an opened copy.~Stop recording and test action on one different copy.~Choose File > Automate > Batch and point Source/Destination to separate folders; inspect output before scaling up.','Consistent edits appear in multiple outputs without changing source files.', 'Automation replays a recorded sequence with limited contextual reasoning.', 'Batch-process three small files into a different output directory.', 'All three have intended dimensions, unique names and originals remain untouched.', 'Recording Save As with absolute paths can cause overwrites.', 'Test one file, then three, before processing hundreds.', 'What should happen before a large batch run?', 'Test small dataset~Delete masters~Assume every image identical',0)
L(10,'Templates and Smart Object replacement','Templates encode stable geometry and interchangeable content. Linked or embedded Smart Objects let designers update a logo or photo without rebuilding layout every time.','Layer > Smart Objects > Replace Contents; File > Place Embedded','Make a product card with a logo Smart Object and editable title layer.~Duplicate the entire PSD as a variant.~Right-click logo Smart Object and choose Replace Contents (or Layer > Smart Objects > Replace Contents).~Pick another same-aspect-ratio PNG and adjust its transform only if required.','Replacement updates the artwork within the preserved layout.', 'A Smart Object container separates content from scene transformations and effects.', 'Produce three branded variations from one saved master.', 'All maintain margins, text hierarchy and shadow style.', 'Replacement assets with different aspect ratios may fit unpredictably.', 'Use standardized asset boxes and document expected proportions.', 'What changes when replacing Smart Object contents?', 'Its source artwork~All Photoshop preferences~Document mode automatically',0)
L(10,'Scripting and supported extensibility boundaries','Actions are built-in; scripting and plugins can automate richer operations but require compatibility checks. UXP is a modern Photoshop extensibility system; legacy script examples may rely on different APIs.','File > Scripts; Plugins menu (when installed)','Open File > Scripts and inspect available built-in commands.~Record a simple Action as a baseline no-code solution.~Review a script only from a trusted local source and determine required Photoshop API/version.~Run scripts on copies, inspect output, and check logs if they fail.','Built-in scripts appear where installed; no third-party tool is needed for the core lessons.', 'Automation code is versioned software and must be treated as a dependency.', 'Write an automation plan identifying which operations need only Actions versus scripting.', 'Plan includes inputs, outputs, safe retries and test conditions.', 'Do not blindly run untrusted JSX or UXP plugin code.', 'Confirm compatibility with version 27.11 before relying on extensions.', 'Which is built into Photoshop without a plugin?', 'Actions panel~Any downloaded automation extension~External cloud API',0)
L(10,'Export As, Quick Export and format decisions','Export is a delivery transformation, not a working-file save. PNG supports transparent graphics; JPEG is lossy and opaque; formats such as WebP depend on receiving system support and export options.','File > Export > Export As; File > Export > Quick Export as PNG','Create a 1080 × 1080 design with text and a transparent margin.~Use File > Export > Export As; compare PNG and JPEG available settings.~Export one PNG with transparency and one JPEG at an appropriate quality.~Reopen both on a contrasting background and inspect size and edge behavior.','PNG preserves transparency; JPEG may show compression and fills transparent areas.', 'Different encoders trade alpha, size and artifact behavior.', 'Deliver one social banner as JPEG and one icon as transparent PNG.', 'Both fit exact requested dimensions and display correctly in target viewer.', 'Do not rely on a flattened PNG to restore text editability.', 'Always inspect exported assets, not only the PSD.', 'Which output is suited to transparent logos?', 'PNG~JPEG~Flattened RGB-only BMP',0)
L(10,'Print, preflight and external application handoffs','Print preflight covers dimensions, bleed, safe area, ICC conversion and fonts; cross-app delivery requires knowing what stays editable. Illustrator paths and Lightroom develop adjustments are not identical to Photoshop layers.','Image > Image Size; Edit > Convert to Profile; File > Print','Open a print-sized PSD and check pixel dimensions with Image > Image Size.~Inspect type layers for missing fonts and links for absent assets.~Use View > Proof Setup > Custom if the print profile is supplied.~Save a versioned master plus a printer-requested delivery copy; communicate bleed/trimming externally.','You have a clean production file and explicit unresolved printer requirements.', 'Production constraints come from a destination system, not one universal print preset.', 'Prepare a 5 × 7 inch poster briefing with print specification checklist.', 'Required profile, bleed, stock and export format are explicitly listed as confirmed or TBD.', 'There is no universally correct CMYK profile for every printer.', 'Use the destination printer’s specification rather than generic recipes.', 'Before converting to CMYK, what is needed?', 'Destination print conditions/profile~A random profile~Only a JPEG filename',0)

# Stage 11
L(11,'GPU, memory and scratch disk triage','Photoshop uses CPU, RAM, GPU and scratch disk for different tasks. Low scratch space, incompatible drivers or an unsupported GPU can cause lag, missing features and crashes.','Edit > Preferences > Performance; Edit > Preferences > Scratch Disks','Open Edit > Preferences > Performance and note Memory Usage and detected GPU.~Confirm the intended scratch disk has substantial free space in Windows File Explorer.~Check Edit > Preferences > Scratch Disks for active locations; do not change them blindly.~Save your file and run one operation that previously lagged while monitoring system load.','You can identify whether memory, scratch space or GPU availability is an obvious bottleneck.', 'Photoshop spills work to disk when memory is inadequate; GPU functions require compatible capability.', 'Make a system inventory with RAM, free scratch space, GPU and driver version.', 'Inventory identifies at least two potential bottlenecks if present.', 'Never clear unknown scratch files while Photoshop is running.', 'Prefer internal fast SSD scratch space and tested graphics drivers.', 'What resource handles spillover when RAM is insufficient?', 'Scratch disk~Font manager~ICC profile',0)
L(11,'Windows VM graphics and feature limitations','A Windows guest needs virtualized DirectX feature support and real GPU capabilities; a Linux host GPU does not automatically become available to Photoshop inside the VM. Official Photoshop system requirements apply to the guest, but hypervisor support varies.','Edit > Preferences > Performance; Help > System Info','Inside the Windows guest, open Help > System Info and record GPU/driver details.~Check Windows Device Manager > Display adapters for guest-presented hardware.~Open Photoshop Performance preferences and verify whether Use Graphics Processor can be selected.~If unsupported, test basic 2D workflows and compare with GPU-dependent ones without assuming pass-through works.','The guest’s actual graphics capability is documented and feature gaps are identified.', 'Virtual GPUs expose only their emulated or passed-through capabilities to the guest OS.', 'Create a VM compatibility report with Windows version, vRAM, DirectX level and Photoshop GPU status.', 'Report distinguishes Adobe minimums from practical VM observations.', 'Do not claim PCIe passthrough or hardware acceleration exists because the host GPU is powerful.', 'Use native Windows hardware when GPU-heavy features are essential and virtualization cannot satisfy them.', 'What GPU matters to Photoshop in a VM?', 'GPU capability exposed to guest~Host specification alone~Linux desktop theme',0)
L(11,'Crashes, resets and safe diagnostics','Crashes can arise from drivers, plugins, fonts, corrupt preferences, scratch exhaustion or damaged documents. Isolate variables before resetting preferences or reinstalling.','Help > System Info; Edit > Preferences > General','Save a separate backup and record the exact crash action.~Note installed Photoshop version via Help > About Photoshop and GPU information via Help > System Info.~Test with nonessential third-party plugins disabled and a small new document.~Only after preserving settings, use documented Reset Preferences on Quit and restart if indicated.','A reproducible trigger or narrowed category is recorded rather than guessed.', 'Controlled experiments change one variable at a time.', 'Write a troubleshooting incident log with symptoms, versions and 3 discriminating tests.', 'Another person could reproduce or eliminate the fault systematically.', 'Resetting preferences may discard custom settings, presets and workspace changes.', 'Export/back up preferences and presets first.', 'What is the first good response to repeated crashes?', 'Record reproducible trigger and configuration~Randomly delete files~Flatten every PSD',0)
L(11,'Missing fonts, links, color shifts and corruption','Broken links and missing fonts change composition. Color shifts arise from unmanaged viewers, missing embedded profiles and proof settings. Corrupt documents require backups rather than optimistic destructive repairs.','Window > Layers; Edit > Color Settings; File > Open','Open a PSD with linked content and inspect link warning icons.~Review text layers for missing fonts and compare a rendered preview with live text.~Check whether image has an embedded profile and inspect color settings; compare in a color-managed viewer.~Save a fresh copy before any relink or font replacement.','You can separate missing-dependency issues from profile/display differences.', 'Documents encode assets, text layout and color interpretation that depend on environment.', 'Produce a dependency checklist for a client PSD and verify linked assets.', 'No silent font substitution or missing photo remains at final export.', 'Assigning a random ICC profile to fix a color mismatch can worsen it.', 'Investigate embed/convert/viewer before changing pixel colors.', 'Which can cause text to reflow on another PC?', 'Missing font~More scratch space~Larger mouse cursor',0)
L(11,'Large files, cache levels and robust recovery','Large PSDs may need PSB; cache levels influence preview performance, and recovery copies are not guaranteed. Separate working copies and periodic verified backups are essential.','Edit > Preferences > Performance; File > Save As','Duplicate a multi-layer PSD and record its file size.~Inspect History & Cache settings in Performance without guessing ideal values.~If a document exceeds PSD format limits, choose PSB when saving and confirm receiving tools accept PSB.~Save versioned files to a backed-up drive and test reopening the most recent revision.','There is at least one recoverable editable copy separate from the active working file.', 'Recovery depends on valid stored content, not merely auto-recovery preferences.', 'Design a 3-2-1-inspired backup plan for working PSDs and source assets.', 'Test restores at least one file and specifies storage locations.', 'Do not assume auto-recovery is a full versioning/backup system.', 'Verify a backup by opening it, not just checking file existence.', 'What is PSB intended for?', 'Large Photoshop documents~Compressed web thumbnails~Vector font installation',0)
L(11,'Offline, account and AI availability','The manual is offline. Photoshop activation and subscription validation may require internet, and Adobe generative services can use cloud processing and credits. Some recent Remove Tool AI processing may use a locally downloaded model on supported hardware.','Help > About Photoshop; Window > Properties; Photoshop Account menu','Inspect the account status and known online requirements before a disconnected session.~For a local editing trial, use non-AI tools such as layers, masks, Clone Stamp and Curves.~If Remove Tool offers on-device versus cloud model options, note required download and hardware availability.~Keep manual files on both Linux and Windows; export website progress as JSON between machines.','You can distinguish editing that works locally from account- and network-dependent operations.', 'Offline reference availability does not imply every Photoshop feature is offline.', 'Create a two-column inventory of offline core editing and online-dependent services.', 'Each listed feature has a specific dependency rather than vague AI label.', 'Do not confuse newly introduced Beta AI Assisted Editor with stable tool behavior.', 'Check credits and terms inside the live Adobe account for actual entitlement.', 'Does a fully offline manual guarantee offline Generative Fill?', 'No~Yes~Only on Linux',0)

# Stage 12
L(12,'Portrait retouching capstone','Combine source preservation, selective healing, tonal dodge/burn, skin-texture protection and delivery review. Quality is judged against a plausible untouched reference.','Healing Brush; Curves adjustment layers; File > Export > Export As','Choose an owned portrait and save a versioned PSD master.~Create cleanup layers for temporary defects and separate Dodge/Burn Curves masks.~Correct tone while preserving subject identity and texture.~Deliver a full-resolution JPEG and a two-panel before/after preview.','Portrait looks more polished but still natural under close inspection.', 'Local changes with editable layers are easier to audit and reverse.', 'Produce a final portrait and written edit log identifying each intervention.', 'At 100% pores and facial texture remain; no obvious healing repetition.', 'Over-retouching is not professional finish.', 'Judge both 100% details and full-face expression.', 'What is the main capstone standard?', 'Natural, reversible improvement~Zero remaining texture~Maximal symmetry',0)
L(12,'Photo restoration capstone','Repair a damaged photo while separating evidenced recovery from imaginative reconstruction. A versioned archive and change log are part of technical quality.','Clone Stamp; Spot Healing; Levels; Layers','Scan or choose owned restoration material; keep immutable original.~Create separate tone and damage repair groups.~Use local cloning/healing with frequent resampling; label missing-content reconstruction as interpreted.~Deliver layered PSD and a contact sheet showing stages.','Damage is reduced without fabricating unmarked historical certainty.', 'Transparent restoration records what is known and what is inferred.', 'Restore five distinct damaged regions with an edit log.', 'Original can be retrieved, and repaired areas remain individually reviewable.', 'Never overwrite the archival scan.', 'Compare before/after at consistent scale and color-management settings.', 'Why record speculative reconstruction?', 'It is not recoverable evidence~It guarantees historical accuracy~It reduces PSD size',0)
L(12,'Product advertisement capstone','Product graphics combine extraction, contact shadows, controlled highlights, typography, color consistency and platform-specific output. Revision flexibility is part of the deliverable.','Select > Select and Mask; Layer Styles; Type Tool; File > Export > Export As','Place an owned product photo over an editable background.~Refine mask, build contact and cast shadows on separate layers.~Add headline, offer and disclaimer text with hierarchy and safe margins.~Deliver a 1080 × 1350 JPEG and a transparent product PNG plus layered PSD.','Product is believable and both outputs have the requested dimensions.', 'Good advertising composition coordinates geometry, light and information.', 'Make a product promo using no paid stock and no flattened working master.', 'Clear subject silhouette, readable text, no clipped shadows at crop edges.', 'Do not make unsupported factual product claims in promotional text.', 'Inspect type on a typical mobile-sized preview.', 'Which PSD organization best supports revisions?', 'Separate product, shadows, text and background~One merged image~Only smart filters',0)
L(12,'Advanced scene-composite capstone','Build a multi-source environment through geometry, lighting and edge integration. The rubric prioritizes perceptual consistency over effect novelty.','File > Place Embedded; Select and Mask; Curves; Smart Filters','Make a scene from at least three independently editable visual assets.~Set a common horizon and one key-light direction.~Integrate each subject with a mask, contact shadow and clipped color correction.~Apply a restrained common grade and export 1800 × 1200 at final quality.','Scene reads as one environment, with believable scale and lighting.', 'Plausibility is the sum of independent visual consistency tests.', 'Deliver source/PSD/export folders and note all source provenance.', 'At 100%, no major halos, contradicted light, floating objects or repeated cloned texture.', 'Do not use color grading to hide incorrect perspective.', 'Turn layers on/off during review to isolate mismatches.', 'What should be audited before final grade?', 'Scale, masks and light~File compression only~Font family count',0)
L(12,'Reusable campaign design capstone','Campaign work demands one coherent design system across formats, predictable text reflow, Smart Object replacement and accurate exports. Production readiness is as important as beauty.','File > Place Embedded; Window > Character; Actions; File > Export > Export As','Create one master design with groups for brand, image, text and background.~Adapt a square 1080 × 1080 version and a vertical 1080 × 1920 version as separate PSD variants.~Replace one Smart Object asset and adjust text safe areas.~Export both and inspect for accidental cropping and legibility.','Two formats share clear visual identity with correct safe zones.', 'A design system expresses stable rules while permitting composition changes.', 'Deliver two sizes, master PSD and a short update guide.', 'Both are legible and editable with consistent typography.', 'Simply scaling the whole square often crops text in vertical output.', 'Design responsive compositions rather than blind resizes.', 'What should remain stable across campaign formats?', 'Design rules, not pixel coordinates~Absolute text position~Every crop box',0)
L(12,'Final assessment: end-to-end editable project','An expert-ready project proves fundamentals, compositing, retouching, typography, color, automation, output and technical handoff in one traceable artifact. Demonstrate decisions as well as mouse actions.','All previously studied menus and panels','Choose a real brief: a three-format campaign built around a retouched original image.~Create an intact source archive and layered PSD with named adjustment, masking and typography groups.~Build one composite/mockup and automate at least one repeatable export or adjustment across three files.~Preflight exports for PNG transparency, JPEG dimensions, color profile, readability and expected recipient needs.~Write a one-page decision log and deliver all variants in a transferable folder.','Every output matches the brief and remains explainable and revisable.', 'Mastery is tested by controlled tradeoffs, diagnostic skill and reproducible delivery.', 'Complete the entire project without relying on paid stock, plugins or Generative Fill.', 'Rubric: 20% preservation/structure; 20% masks/composite; 20% tonal/color; 15% typography/layout; 15% delivery; 10% documentation. Target ≥85% with no critical failure.', 'An attractive JPEG alone does not prove professional Photoshop competence.', 'Peer-review the layered PSD, not just exported appearance.', 'Which evidence best demonstrates complete mastery?', 'Editable project, verified outputs and decision log~Only one attractive thumbnail~Shortcut memorization alone',0)

# Editorial version-specific enrichments cross-checked against Adobe's 2026 documentation.
light=next(x for x in LESSONS if x['id']=='s04l01')
light['concept']+=' In Photoshop 27.10 and later, Light is a dedicated editable adjustment layer providing Exposure, Contrast, Highlights, Shadows, Whites and Blacks; it is not the same panel as traditional Curves.'
light['steps'].append('Add Layer > New Adjustment Layer > Light in Photoshop 27.11; test Highlights −15 and Shadows +15 in Properties, then toggle visibility and compare with the Curves result.')
light['tip']+=' For six basic tonal controls try Light; for exact curve shapes and channel control use Curves.'
edge=next(x for x in LESSONS if x['id']=='s03l04')
edge['concept']+=' Photoshop 27.11 adds AI-powered Enhance edge refinement; availability or processing conditions should be checked in the live selection workflow.'
edge['steps'].append('If the 27.11 Enhance edge control is available, compare its result with standard Select and Mask refinement; retain the version with fewer fringes rather than assuming AI is always better.')
linked=next(x for x in LESSONS if x['id']=='s10l01')
linked['steps'].append('After saving the PSD, inspect File > Package as Adobe’s built-in option to gather linked source assets, and verify packaged files before handoff.')
assert len(LESSONS)==75,len(LESSONS)
